              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_pages"} Related pages
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %em index
            was originally defined
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#IdeaForDifferentialOperators"} For elliptic differential operators
          %p
            as an invariant correction of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
            of such an operator (namely corrected by the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cokernel"} cokernel
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#ForDiracOperators"} For Dirac operators
          %p
            where it coincides with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetric+quantum+mechanics"} supersymmetric quantum mechanics
          %p
            More generally the resulting notion is abstractly characterized as being the pairing operation (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"}> composition
            )
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#GeneralIdeaInKKTheory"} in KK-theory
              \.
          %p
            Even more generally, in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology+theory"} generalized cohomology theory
            indices are given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genera"} genera
            and universal
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation+in+generalized+cohomology"} orientation in generalized cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+genus"} elliptic genus
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+cohomology"} elliptic cohomology
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten+genus"} Witten genus
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tmf"} tmf
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genus"} genus
            for more on this generalized notion of indices.
          %p
            There is also a quite general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microlocal+formulation+of+index+theory"} microlocal formulation of index theory
            due to Kashiwara and Schapira.
          %h3#IdeaForDifferentialOperators For elliptic differential and Fredholm operators
          %p
            The
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytical+index"} analytical index
            of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+operator"} elliptic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+operator"} differential operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \colon \Gamma(E_1) \to \Gamma(E_2)
            is defined to be the the difference between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            of its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
            and that of its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cokernel"} cokernel
          %p
            One reason why this is an interesting invariant of an elliptic differential operator is that when deforming the operator by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+operator"} compact operator
            then the dimension of both the kernel and the cokernel may change, but their difference remains the same. Hence one may think of the analytic index as a “corrected” version of its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
          %p
            On the other hand, the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+index"} topological index
            of an elliptic differential operator
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cup+product"} cup product
            of its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+character"} Chern character
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Todd+class"} Todd class
            of the base manifold with its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+class"} fundamental class
          %p
            More generally such analytic and topological indices are defined for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fredholm+operators"} Fredholm operators
          %p
            The
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Atiyah-Singer+index+theorem"} Atiyah-Singer index theorem
            assert that these two notios of index are in fact equal.
          %h3#ForDiracOperators For Dirac operators
          %p
            If the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fredholm+operator"} Fredholm operator
            in question happens to be a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            (such as that encoding the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamics"} dynamics
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinning+particle"} spinning particle
            or more generally the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supercharge"} supercharge
            of a system in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetric+quantum+mechanics"} supersymmetric quantum mechanics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            coincides with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            of this quantum mechanical system, namely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super-trace"} super-trace
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heat+kernel"} heat kernel
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi t
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(-t D^2)
            of the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laplace+operator"} Laplace operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^2
            (
            %a{:href => "#BerlineGetzlerVergne04"}> Berline-Getzler-Vergne 04
            ).
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,g)
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              a smooth
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+vector+bundle"} super vector bundle
              and indeed a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clifford+module+bundle"} Clifford module bundle
              over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    D \colon \Gamma(X,\mathcal{E}) \to \Gamma(X, \mathcal{E})
            %p
              with components (with respect to the
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+vector+space"} grading
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %mo [
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mn 0
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mi D
                                %mo −
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mi D
                                %mo +
                            %mtd
                              %mn 0
                      %mo ]
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    D =
                    \\left[
                    \\array{
                    0 &amp; D^-
                    \\\
                    D^+ &amp; 0
                    }
                    \\right]
                    \\,,
            %p
              where
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi D
                        %mo −
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi D
                        %mo +
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^- = (D^+)^\ast
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^+
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fredholm+operator"} Fredholm operator
              and its index is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supertrace"} supertrace
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heat+kernel"} heat kernel
              of
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi D
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^2
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi ind
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mi D
                              %mo +
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≔
                            %mi dim
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi ker
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mi D
                              %mo +
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo −
                            %mi dim
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi coker
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mi D
                              %mo +
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mi dim
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi ker
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mi D
                              %mo +
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo −
                            %mi dim
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi ker
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mi D
                              %mo −
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mi sTr
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi ker
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi D
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mi sTr
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi exp
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mi t
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %msup
                              %mi D
                              %mn 2
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mo ∀
                            %mi t
                            %mo &gt;
                            %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    ind(D^+) &amp; \coloneqq dim(ker(D^+)) - dim(coker(D^+))
                    \\\
                    \&amp; = dim(ker(D^+)) - dim(ker(D^-))
                    \\\
                    \&amp; = sTr(ker(D))
                    \\\
                    \&amp; = sTr( \exp(-t \, D^2) ) \;\;\; \forall t \gt 0
                    \\end{aligned}
                    \\,.
          %p
            This appears as (
            %a{:href => "BerlineGetzlerVergne04"}> Berline-Getzler-Vergne 04, prop. 3.48, prop. 3.50
            ), based on (
            %a{:href => "#MacKeanSinger67"}> MacKean-Singer 67
            ).
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              The last step here follows from an argument which is as simple as it is paramount whenever anything involves
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            %p
              the point is that if a (hermitean) operator
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supercharge"} supercharge
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi D
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H = D^2
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eigenstates"} eigenstates
              appear in “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supermultiplet"}> supermultiplet
              ” pairs of the same eigenvalue: if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\psi\rangle
              has
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eigenvalue"} eigenvalue
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \gt 0
              under
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi ψ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mo ≠
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D |\psi\rangle \neq 0
                  (since
                  = succeed ");" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi D
                          %mi D
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi ψ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mo =
                          %mi H
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi ψ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mo =
                          %mi E
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi ψ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mo ≠
                          %mn 0
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D D |\psi\rangle = H |\psi\rangle = E |\psi\rangle \neq 0
              %li
                %p
                  also
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi ψ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D |\psi\rangle
                  has eignevalue
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                  (since
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi H
                          %mo ,
                          %mi D
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mo =
                          %mn 0
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [H,D] = 0
            %p
              Therefore all eigenstates for non-vanishing eigenvalues appear in pairs whose members have opposite sign under the supertrace. So only states with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H |\psi\rangle = 0
              contribute to the supertrace. But if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              are hermitean operators for a non-degenerate inner product, then it follows that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⇔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (D^2 |\psi\rangle = 0) \Leftrightarrow (D|\psi\rangle = 0)
              and hence these are precisely the states which are also annihilated by the supercharge (are in the kernel of
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              = succeed "." do
                %em supersymmetric states
            %p
              On these now the weight
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi exp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi t
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(- t D^2) = 1
              and hence the supertrace over this “Euclidean propagator” simply counts the number of supersymmetric states, signed by their fermion number.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              If one thinks of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^2
              as the time-evolution
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
              of a system of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetric+quantum+mechanics"} supersymmetric quantum mechanics
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supercharge"} supercharge
              on the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ker
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ker(D)
              is the space of supersymmetric
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi exp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi t
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(-t \, D^2)
              is the Euclidean time evolution operator and its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supertrace"} supertrace
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
              of the system. Hence we have the translation
            %ul
              %li index = partition function .
            %p
              This kind of argument appears throughout supersymmetric quantum field theory. In dimension 2 it controls the nature of the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten+genus"} Witten genus
          %h3#GeneralIdeaInKKTheory General abstract definition in KK-theory
          %p
            The abstract
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal
            characterization of indices is: the index is the
            %em pairing
            in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-theory"} E-theory
          %p
            More in detail, by the discussion there
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-theory"}> E-theory
            ) is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi KK
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KK
            which is the additive and split exact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
            of the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2AAlg"} C*Alg
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
            at the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+operators"} compact operators
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            the base
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebra"} C*-algebra
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            in this category have the following equivalent meaning:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C} \to A
                are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+K-theory"} operator K-cohomology
                classes which are represented by “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundles"} vector bundles
                over the space represented by
                = succeed "\u201D," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+modules"} Hilbert modules
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                over
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to \mathcal{C}
                are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-homology"} K-homology
                classes which are represented by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fredholm+operators"} Fredholm operators
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ind
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi D
                        %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi E
                      %mi A
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi D
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi KK
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      ind(D_E)
                      \\;\colon\;
                      \\mathbb{C} \stackrel{E}{\to} A \stackrel{D}{\to} \mathbb{C}
                      \\;\;\;\;
                      \\in KK(\mathbb{C}, \mathbb{C}) \simeq \mathbb{Z}
              %p
                in the category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi KK
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KK
                (hence the Kasparov product) is the
                %em index
                of the Fredholm operator
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                twisted by
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
          %p
            More generally, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is some other chosen base
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebra"} C*-algebra
            then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi KK
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KK(A,B)
            is the group of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fredholm+operators"} Fredholm operators
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+module"} Hilbert module
            bundles over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebra"} C*-algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ind
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mo ∘
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi KK
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi KK
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi KK
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  ind \coloneqq \circ_{\mathbb{C}, A, B} \;\colon\;
                  KK(\mathbb{C},A) \times KK(A,B) \to KK(\mathbb{C}, B)
          %p
            to be the index map relative
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "#Schick05"} Schick 05, section 6
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mishchenko-Fomenko+index+theorem"} Mishchenko-Fomenko index theorem
            applies to.
          %p
            And hence even more generally one may regard
            %em any
            composition in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi KK
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KK
            as as a generalized index map. Via the universal characterizatin of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi KK
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KK
            itself, this then gives a fundamental and general abstract characterization of the notion of index:
          %p
            %em
              The index pairing is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
