                  %em A short proof of the Atiyah-Singer index theorem
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://math.northwestern.edu/~getzler/Papers/local.pdf"} pdf
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D.+Quillen"}> D. Quillen
              ,
              = succeed "." do
                %em Superconnection character forms and the Cayley transform
              %p (1988), no. 2, 211–238
          %p
            For the more general discussion of indices of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+complexes"} elliptic complexes
            see
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Gilkey"}> Peter Gilkey
              ,
              %em Invariance theory, the heat equation, and the Atiyah-Singer index theorem
              (
              %a{:href => "http://pages.uoregon.edu/gilkey/dirPDF/InvarianceTheory1Ed.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            An explicit formula in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Weil+theory"} Chern-Weil theory
            for indices of differential operators on
            = succeed "-bundles" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+modules"} Hilbert modules
          %ul#Schick05
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Schick"}> Thomas Schick
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi L
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2
                  \-index, KK-theory, and connections
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0306171"} arXiv:math/0306171
          %p
            A standard textbook account in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
            is in section 24.1 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bruce+Blackadar"}> Bruce Blackadar
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-Theory+for+Operator+Algebras"} K-Theory for Operator Algebras
        .revisedby
